Done. `python selftest2.py . 23_glob` reports `ref PASS` (fresh-code task, so no `seed/`).

**23_glob** — a gitignore-style path matcher (`globmatch.py` with `compile_rules`/`is_ignored`/`RuleError`). The spec pins down, in full and self-contained: escaped-aware trailing-space stripping (odd-backslash rule, leading spaces significant), comments/blanks, leading `!` negation with last-matching-rule-wins, trailing `/` dir-only rules, leading-`/` and internal-`/` anchoring, `*`/`?`/`[...]` with ranges, `[!...]` negation, first-`]` literal, escaped chars in classes, reversed ranges, `**` whole-segment = zero-or-more components, escaped `/` semantics, empty patterns/segments, and the two invalid-pattern cases raising `RuleError`. Directories are indicated by a trailing `/` on the path argument, and an ignored ancestor directory short-circuits to "ignored" (negation can't re-include inside an ignored dir — like git). The grader combines ~130 hand-written cases covering every rule plus a seeded (seed 23) differential test of 10,000 rule-set/path evaluations against a simple structured-rule reference matcher implemented inside `test.py` (deliberately independent of the agent's text parsing).

The selftest run also caught three of my own spec mistakes during authoring, which I corrected: a differential renderer bug (`**` rendered as `*`), and two hand-case expectations that ignored the ancestor-directory rule (`**/x` does ignore `a/x/b`, since directory `a/x` matched), plus `[!]` being unterminated rather than a negated-`]` class.
